{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":2882423,"items":[{"DOI":"10.4324\/9780203774427-12","title":["How Individual Emotional Episodes Feed Collective Memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9780203774427-12","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2013,6,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2013,6,17]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory of Political Events"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6380758","title":["Integrating Large Language Models with Autonomous Search Agents for Advanced Information Retrieval"],"author":[{"given":"Noah","family":"Gates","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6380758","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has revolutionized natural language processing, yet these models remain fundamentally constrained by their reliance on static training corpora, resulting in knowledge cutoffs, factual hallucinations, and an inability to access real-time or proprietary information. This paper addresses these critical limitations by proposing a novel architectural framework for integrating LLMs with autonomous search agents to create hybrid systems capable of advanced information retrieval. Moving beyond simplistic retrievalaugmented generation (RAG) approaches, this research introduces a dynamic orchestration layer wherein LLMs function not merely as answer generators but as strategic reasoning cores that decompose complex queries, formulate search intentions, and delegate execution to specialized autonomous agents. These agents, equipped with tool-use capabilities, navigate heterogeneous information landscapes-including public web repositories, deep web databases, and structured knowledge bases-through iterative search, filtering, and evidence accumulation. The framework incorporates a bidirectional feedback loop: search results inform and refine the LLM's reasoning process, while the LLM's evolving understanding guides subsequent search strategies. We evaluate the integrated system against traditional LLM baselines and simple RAG implementations using benchmarks that test multi-step reasoning, temporal awareness, and information synthesis across disparate sources. Results demonstrate significant improvements in factual accuracy (reducing hallucinations by approximately 40%), query completion rates for complex multi-source requests, and the ability to answer time-sensitive questions requiring upto-date information. This work contributes a principled methodology for creating transparent, verifiable, and dynamically knowledgeable AI systems, with implications for academic research, enterprise intelligence, and the democratization of advanced information access. The proposed integration paradigm establishes a foundation for the next generation of intelligent information retrieval systems that combine the reasoning depth of LLMs with the breadth and timeliness of autonomous search.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1093\/oso\/9780192895363.003.0001","title":["Introduction"],"author":[{"given":"Kathrin","family":"Bachleitner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/oso\/9780192895363.003.0001","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,3,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,3,18]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory in International Relations"]},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781316809600.006","title":["Study on Russian Collective Memory: Methodology"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781316809600.006","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"container-title":["Collective Remembering"]},{"DOI":"10.1504\/ijci.2014.064850","title":["Collective memory based on semantic annotation among economic intelligence actors"],"author":[{"given":"Bensattalah","family":"Aissa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Fahima","family":"Nader","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Rachid","family":"Chalal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1504\/ijci.2014.064850","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2014]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2014]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Collaborative Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2022.104334","title":["Contrast coding choices in a decade of mixed models"],"author":[{"given":"Laurel","family":"Brehm","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Phillip M.","family":"Alday","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2022.104334","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,8]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/bs.pbr.2022.07.001","title":["Collective memory in the digital age"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/bs.pbr.2022.07.001","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"container-title":["Progress in Brain Research","Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2010.06.001","title":["The inverse list length effect: A challenge for pure exemplar models of recognition memory"],"author":[{"given":"Simon","family":"Dennis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Allison","family":"Chapman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2010.06.001","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2010,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2010,10]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2006.10.003","title":["Models of relevant cue integration in name retrieval"],"author":[{"given":"Luigi","family":"Lombardi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Giuseppe","family":"Sartori","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2006.10.003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2007,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2007,7]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.7551\/mitpress\/9173.003.0015","title":["Persistence of Consolidated Collective Memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7551\/mitpress\/9173.003.0015","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2012,2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2012,2,24]]},"container-title":["Individual and Collective Memory Consolidation"]},{"DOI":"10.1057\/9780230621633_7","title":["Palestinian Women and Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Hanita","family":"Brand","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1057\/9780230621633_7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2009]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2009]]},"container-title":["Palestinian Collective Memory and National Identity"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2025.104713","title":["Structural priming in humans and large language models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1210-7011","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Arabella","family":"Sinclair","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anastasia","family":"Klimovich-Gray","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jaap","family":"Jumelet","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nika","family":"Adamian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Agnieszka","family":"Konopka","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2025.104713","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/s0079-6123(22)00183-2","title":["Preface: Collective memory comes of age"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/s0079-6123(22)00183-2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"container-title":["Progress in Brain Research","Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9783110772715-004","title":["3 Framing Collective Memory and Literature: Some Theoretical Observations"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9783110772715-004","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,5]]},"container-title":["Cajun Literature and Cajun Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781315107608-11","title":["Collective Responsibility and Collective Obligations Without Collective Moral Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Gunnar","family":"Bj\u00f6rnsson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781315107608-11","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,4,19]]},"container-title":["The Routledge Handbook of Collective Responsibility"]},{"DOI":"10.1093\/oso\/9780190860554.003.0003","title":["Justice, Acknowledgment, and Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Andrew","family":"Valls","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/oso\/9780190860554.003.0003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2018,9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2018,9,20]]},"abstract":"<p>In regime transitions, a number of mechanisms are utilized to memorialize the past and to reject the ideas associated with human rights abused of the prior regime. This is often done through truth commissions, apologies, memorials, museums, changes in place names, national holidays, and other symbolic measures. In the United States, some efforts along these lines have been undertaken, but on the whole they have been very limited and inadequate. In addition, many symbols and memorials associated with the past, such as Confederate monuments and the Confederate Battle Flag, continue to be displayed. Hence while some progress has been made on these issues, much more needs to be done.<\/p>","container-title":["Oxford Scholarship Online"]},{"DOI":"10.7551\/mitpress\/9173.003.0016","title":["Loss of Unconsolidated Collective Memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7551\/mitpress\/9173.003.0016","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2012,2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2012,2,24]]},"container-title":["Individual and Collective Memory Consolidation"]},{"DOI":"10.4135\/9781506337098.n3","title":["Collective Teacher Efficacy (1.57)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4135\/9781506337098.n3","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017]]},"container-title":["Collaborative Leadership"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2025.104676","title":["LLMs as models for analogical reasoning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-4800-9168","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sam","family":"Musker","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alex","family":"Duchnowski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rapha\u00ebl","family":"Milli\u00e8re","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ellie","family":"Pavlick","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2025.104676","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5097309","title":["Collaborative Large Language Models for Task Allocation in Construction Robots"],"author":[{"given":"Samuel A.","family":"Prieto","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Borja","family":"Garc\u00eda de Soto","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5097309","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9781800735965-012","title":["Conclusion. A Collaborative Memory Not Yet Achieved"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9781800735965-012","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,31]]},"container-title":["Towards a Collaborative Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.tate.2024.104802","title":["Conceptualizing reflection-for-action: Empowering teachers as agents of collaborative and collective transformation"],"author":[{"given":"Elif","family":"Burhan-Horasanl\u0131","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Deniz Orta\u00e7tepe","family":"Hart","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.tate.2024.104802","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,12]]},"container-title":["Teaching and Teacher Education"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5433894","title":["Structured Path Resonance Loop (SPRL): A Native Collaborative Language Interface for Large Reasoning Models (LRMs)"],"author":[{"given":"Xinliang","family":"Wei","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5433894","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.36315\/2024v2end055","title":["Unleashing personalized education using large language models in online collaborative settings"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36315\/2024v2end055","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,15]]},"container-title":["Education and New Developments 2024 \u2013 Volume 2"]},{"DOI":"10.5117\/9789463728744-2","title":["Collective Memory and Unremembering"],"author":[{"given":"Paul M.M.","family":"Doolan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5117\/9789463728744-2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,1]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory and the Dutch East Indies"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2005.08.004","title":["Timeless memory: Evidence against temporal distinctiveness models of short-term memory for serial order"],"author":[{"given":"Stephan","family":"Lewandowsky","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Gordon D.A.","family":"Brown","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Tarryn","family":"Wright","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Lisa M.","family":"Nimmo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2005.08.004","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2006,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2006,1]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1093\/oxfordhb\/9780195339550.013.0007","title":["Can Memory Be Collective?"],"author":[{"given":"Anna","family":"Green","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/oxfordhb\/9780195339550.013.0007","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2012,9,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2012,9,18]]},"abstract":"<p>This article explains the collectivity of memory. Memory, in all its guises, has been at the heart of historical inquiry over the past three decades. Cultural and social historians, sociologists, social psychologists, and those working in cultural studies and literary criticism have generated a significant body of work exploring both individual autobiographical memory and collective, public memory. Interest in the subject of collective remembrance, initially focusing upon the social and cultural forms through which the violent and repressive history of the twentieth century were recalled and commemorated, has developed over time into a broader, interdisciplinary field focusing upon memory. The term \u201cmemory\u201d has now expanded to encompass all these forms of historical consciousness, a development that has received a less-than-enthusiastic response from those historians who define conventional history by its goals of objectivity and truth, as opposed to the subjectivity and partiality of memory. Discussion on personal and collective memory and social identities conclude this article.<\/p>","container-title":["Oxford Handbooks Online"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003374527-10","title":["On the ambivalences of fandom's collective memory"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9720-6595","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wojciech","family":"Wo\u017aniak","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003374527-10","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,22]]},"container-title":["Football, Fandom and Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1006\/jmla.2000.2716","title":["Models of Impaired Lexical Access in Speech Production"],"author":[{"given":"Dan","family":"Foygel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Gary S","family":"Dell","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1006\/jmla.2000.2716","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2000,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2000,8]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2006.05.005","title":["From prototypes to caricatures: Geometrical models for concept typicality"],"author":[{"given":"Eef","family":"Ameel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Gert","family":"Storms","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2006.05.005","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2006,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2006,10]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1023\/a:1026572712714","title":["Collaborative Support for Informal Information in Collective Memory Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Mark S.","family":"Ackerman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"David W.","family":"McDonald","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1023\/a:1026572712714","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2000,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2000,10]]},"container-title":["Information Systems Frontiers"]},{"DOI":"10.5040\/9780755625321.ch-002","title":["Gender and Collective Memory: Women and Representation at Auschwitz"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5040\/9780755625321.ch-002","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2010]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2010]]},"container-title":["Memorializing the Holocaust"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781315702179-12","title":["Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Arthur G.","family":"Neal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781315702179-12","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2018,4,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2018,4,17]]},"container-title":["National Trauma and Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/s0749-596x(02)00503-x","title":["Semantic facilitation and semantic interference in word translation: Implications for models of lexical access in language production"],"author":[{"given":"I","family":"Bloem","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/s0749-596x(02)00503-x","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2003,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2003,4]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5767563","title":["Two-Faced Social Agents: Context Collapse in Role-Conditioned Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Vikram","family":"K Suresh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5767563","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2026.104751","title":["Manipulating language models\u2019 training data to study syntactic constraint learning: The case of English passivization"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-6238-0842","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Cara Su-Yi","family":"Leong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0435-6912","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tal","family":"Linzen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2026.104751","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-2008-3.ch003","title":["Transforming Healthcare with Large Language Models and AI Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-3705-1681","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ravikumar","family":"R. N.","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Marwadi University, Rajkot, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-9064-2091","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Aarthi","family":"S.","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Marwadi University, Rajkot, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Izzatbek Rejapov","family":"Olimbaevich","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Mamun University, Khiva, Uzbekistan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sultonova","family":"Mashkhura","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Urgench State University Named After Abu Raykhan Beruni, Urgench, Uzbekistan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Saodat","family":"Musayeva","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Termez University of Economics and Service, Termez, Uzbekistan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-2008-3.ch003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,18]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The chapter \u201cTransforming Healthcare with Large Language Models and AI Agents\u201d explores how technologies like GPT-3 and advanced AI agents are reshaping healthcare. These tools can process massive volumes of medical data to provide real-time insights, aiding clinical decision-making and improving patient outcomes. AI agents enhance patient engagement, assist in diagnosis, and support treatment planning. Key applications include boosting diagnostic accuracy, automating administrative processes, enabling virtual care, and advancing precision medicine. The chapter also addresses critical challenges such as data privacy, algorithmic bias, and the importance of transparent AI governance. It offers practical recommendations to mitigate these issues and ensure responsible AI deployment. Designed for healthcare professionals, researchers, and policymakers, this chapter provides a comprehensive guide to harnessing the power of LLMs and AI agents to transform healthcare delivery and medical research effectively and ethically.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics","Advanced Interdisciplinary Applications of Large Language Models and AI Agents"]},{"DOI":"10.1057\/9780230621633_1","title":["Introduction: Collective Memory and the Palestinian Experience"],"author":[{"given":"Meir","family":"Litvak","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1057\/9780230621633_1","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2009]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2009]]},"container-title":["Palestinian Collective Memory and National Identity"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2020.104092","title":["Best practice guidance for linear mixed-effects models in psychological science"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1184-1908","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lotte","family":"Meteyard","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Robert A.I.","family":"Davies","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2020.104092","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,6]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.21275\/sr25048081833","title":["Building Effective AI Agents with Large Language Models: Workflows, Design Patterns, and Best Practices"],"author":[{"given":"Sunil","family":"Karthik Kota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21275\/sr25048081833","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,30]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,30]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Science and Research (IJSR)"]},{"DOI":"10.5117\/9789463728744_ch01","title":["Collective Memory and Unremembering"],"author":[{"given":"Paul M.M.","family":"Doolan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5117\/9789463728744_ch01","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Maurice Halbwachs demonstrated that memory is shaped by social forces.\n\t\t\t\t\tI argue that collective memory is mediated in symbolic representations.\n\t\t\t\t\tNational collective memory is unstable and open to revision and is a site\n\t\t\t\t\tfor dispute between alternative representations. Changes in media lead\n\t\t\t\t\tto changes in collective memory. The trauma within collective memory\n\t\t\t\t\tis passed down through generations. I argue that collective memory\n\t\t\t\t\tis not a process of remembering and forgetting, but remembering and\n\t\t\t\t\tunremembering. Unremembering is a process of concealment. Literature\n\t\t\t\t\tis significant in shaping collective memory, but so too are film, memoir,\n\t\t\t\t\tpublic media like newspapers and television, as well as works of historical\n\t\t\t\t\tscholarship. I end this chapter with a factual summary of the Indonesian\n\t\t\t\t\tWar of Independence.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Collective Memory and the Dutch East Indies"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4895723","title":["Unveiling the Collective Behaviors of Large Language Model-Based Autonomous Agents in an Online Community: A Social Network Study"],"author":[{"given":"Huiru","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhenhua","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ming","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4895723","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/1878537.1878544","title":["Docking agent-based simulation of collective emotion to equation-based models and interactive agents"],"author":[{"given":"Stefan","family":"Rank","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Austrian Research Institute for Artificial Intelligence, Vienna, Austria"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/1878537.1878544","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2010,4,11]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2010,4,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2010,4,11]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2010 Spring Simulation Multiconference"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-981-95-4632-9_5","title":["Agents and Workflows"],"author":[{"given":"Jingsong Shawn","family":"Yu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yazhi","family":"Yao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-981-95-4632-9_5","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Intelligent Language Services"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2013.04.004","title":["Asymmetric cross-domain interference between two working memory tasks: Implications for models of working memory"],"author":[{"given":"Candice C.","family":"Morey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Richard D.","family":"Morey","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Madeleine","family":"van der Reijden","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Margot","family":"Holweg","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2013.04.004","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2013,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2013,10]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.174494725.56070193\/v1","title":["Retrieval-Native Language Models: Integrating Parametric and Vector Memory with Bayesian Attention"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-1914-8184","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"M Mostagir","family":"Bhuiyan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.174494725.56070193\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,18]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large pre-trained language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable ability to encode vast world knowledge in their parameters, but this static, implicit storage poses challenges for keeping models up-to-date and providing provenance for factual claims [1]. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) methods address these issues by equipping LLMs with external non-parametric memory (typically a vector database of documents) that can be queried at inference time [1][2]. However, in standard RAG pipelines the retrieval module is an external add-on, not a native part of the model architecture. We propose Retrieval-Native Language Models (RLLMs), a new paradigm that treats vector-based memory as a first-class component of the model. RLLM introduces a unified architecture with three channels of knowledge: (1) Parametric memory in the pretrained model weights (static knowledge); (2) a live retrieval channel that shares an embedding space with the generator for continual intake of external vectors; and (3) an internal Bayesian attention router that dynamically adjusts how information flows from retrieval into generation based on the estimated utility of retrieved results (e.g. relevance, correctness, recency). By tightly integrating retrieval into the model's multi-head attention layers and training the retriever and generator jointly, RLLMs align the model's representations with the memory index and enable endto-end learning of when to trust retrieved evidence. We present a theoretical analysis of how unified embedding alignment and multi-channel attention improve efficiency and reduce hallucination. We describe the RLLM architecture in detail, including pseudo-code for the retrieval-augmented attention mechanism and a Bayesian feedback loop that updates routing weights in light of retrieval success or failure signals. In experiments on knowledge-intensive QA benchmarks (NaturalQuestions, HotpotQA) and a streaming enterprise wiki scenario, RLLM outperforms traditional RAG baselines. It achieves higher answer accuracy with 30% fewer parameters than fully-parametric models by leveraging its vector memory, and quickly adapts to new information with minimal latency. This work demonstrates that making retrieval a native component of LLMs yields significant gains in knowledge access, interpretability, and continual learning, suggesting a new direction for building more flexible and powerful language models.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.177126294.41037856\/v1","title":["Dynamic KV Injection: An Attention-Level User Memory System for Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-6427-1170","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ma","family":"MingJian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.177126294.41037856\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,16]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Current memory augmentation approaches for Large Language Models (LLMs), such as Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), inject retrieved content by concatenating it to the input prompt. This paradigm consumes valuable context window tokens and requires careful prompt engineering. We propose Dynamic KV Injection (DKI), a training-free, inference-time user-level cross-session memory system that injects Key-Value representations directly into the attention mechanism without consuming prompt tokens. Scope Definition: DKI is designed specifically for user-level memory (personal preferences, session history, user context), not for external knowledge bases or public data retrieval. This focused scope enables architectural simplifications and reduces position encoding risks. Our key contributions include: (1) a paradigm shift from token-level to attention-level memory injection; (2) Memory Influence Scaling (MIS) with continuous strength control \u03b1 \u2208 [0, 1]; (3) Query-Conditioned Memory Projection using FiLM-style modulation; (4) Dual-Factor Gating combining uncertainty and relevance signals; (5) Hybrid Injection Strategy: negative-position K\/V injection for stable preferences + suffix-prompt injection (explicit token-level history reference) for dynamic session history; and (6) Standardized Plugin Architecture enabling configuration-based deployment. We introduce the Attention Budget Reallocation Hypothesis: in reasoning-intensive tasks, the marginal benefit of releasing token budget exceeds the marginal cost of increased attention computation. Preliminary experiments on PersonaChat (n=500) show comparable memory recall (86.2% vs 87.3%) with 43.7% reduction in subsequent-turn latency; DKI significantly outperforms RAG by +10-16% at user context lengths &gt;2500 tokens, strongly supporting the budget reallocation hypothesis.<\/jats:p>"},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,2,7]]},"abstract":"<p>Chapter 4 asks, \u201cWhat can deviant forms of remembering tell us about the normative project of collective remembrance?\u201d This chapter draws on newspaper articles, blogs, and interviews to explore the world of \u201cmemory deviants\u201d who refuse to engage in \u2018socially responsible\u201d forms of remembering and the identity constructions implicit in them. This chapter investigates the limits of constructing a consensus-driven collective memory through the lens of dissenting voices, particularly those of the people meant to identify with revised collective narratives.<\/p>","DOI":"10.7312\/columbia\/9780231177580.003.0005","title":["Memory Deviants"],"author":[{"given":"Robyn","family":"Autry","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2019,1,24]]},"container-title":["Desegregating the Past"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,2,7]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7312\/columbia\/9780231177580.003.0005"},{"DOI":"10.1093\/acprof:oso\/9780199978267.003.0003","title":["Collective Intentions"],"author":[{"given":"Raimo","family":"Tuomela","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/acprof:oso\/9780199978267.003.0003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2013,9,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2013,9,16]]},"container-title":["Social Ontology"]},{"DOI":"10.21437\/icslp.2002-135","title":["Subset languages for conversing with collaborative interface agents"],"author":[{"given":"Candace L.","family":"Sidner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Clifton","family":"Forlines","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21437\/icslp.2002-135","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2002,9,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2002,9,16]]},"container-title":["7th International Conference on Spoken Language Processing (ICSLP 2002)"]},{"DOI":"10.1017\/cbo9780511626999.011","title":["How Does Collective Memory Create a Sense of the Collective?"],"author":[{"given":"Alan J.","family":"Lambert","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Laura Nesse","family":"Scherer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Chad","family":"Rogers","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Larry","family":"Jacoby","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/cbo9780511626999.011","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2009,5,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2009,5,29]]},"container-title":["Memory in Mind and Culture"]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3749421.3749426","title":["Capabilities and Opportunities of Language Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3749421.3749426","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,11]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,11]]},"container-title":["Multi-LLM Agent Collaborative Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-031-89103-8_7","title":["Enhancing Accuracy and\u00a0Explainability in\u00a0Anomaly Classification with\u00a0Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Roberto","family":"Santana","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-031-89103-8_7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Communications in Computer and Information Science","Advances in Explainability, Agents, and Large Language Models"]},{"DOI":"10.1080\/09658210701810187","title":["Performance and process in collective and individual memory: The role of social decision schemes and memory bias in collective memory"],"author":[{"given":"Lyn M.","family":"Van Swol","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/09658210701810187","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2008,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2008,4]]},"container-title":["Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.5040\/9798216415787.ch-6","title":["Out of the Archives: Fostering Collaborative Environments for Language Revitalization"],"author":[{"given":"Gabriella","family":"Reznowski","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Norma A.","family":"Joseph","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5040\/9798216415787.ch-6","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2011]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2011]]},"container-title":["Tribal Libraries, Archives, and Museums"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9780203774427-21","title":["Distortions of Collective Memory: How Groups Flatter and Deceive Themselves"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9780203774427-21","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2013,6,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2013,6,17]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory of Political Events"]},{"DOI":"10.4337\/9781789901917.00030","title":["Functional collective action dilemma and collaborative management"],"author":[{"given":"Heewon","family":"Lee","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4337\/9781789901917.00030","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,2,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,2,12]]},"container-title":["Handbook of Collaborative Public Management"]},{"DOI":"10.1057\/9781137450159_9","title":["Heysel and its Symbolic Value in Europe\u2019s Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Clemens","family":"Kech","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1057\/9781137450159_9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2015]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2015]]},"container-title":["European Football and Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.177155639.91955081\/v1","title":["Small Language Models as Autonomous Agents: Architectures, Retrieval Strategies, and Applications"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-7594-9432","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hammaad","family":"Rizwan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Informatics Institute of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Deshan","family":"Sumanathilaka","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Informatics Institute of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Venkatesh","family":"Raju","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"R&D"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.177155639.91955081\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,20]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>With the advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) as autonomous agents, the number of potential applications has increased significantly. However, the most powerful LLMs demand substantial computational resources, which in turn heightens reliance on cloud-based services. This raises concerns regarding both privacy and cost per request, underscoring the need for the development and deployment of Smaller Language Models (SLMs) as agents. This systematic review provides a comprehensive overview of recent advancements in SLMs as agents and examines state-of-the-art retrieval mechanisms designed to mitigate hallucinations. It summarizes the current state of these technologies and serves as a valuable resource for researchers seeking to identify effective SLMs and methods for improving the factuality and consistency of model outputs. Furthermore, the review highlights existing limitations in current systems and proposes potential directions for future research.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9780295746234-005","title":["1 The Construction of Collective Memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9780295746234-005","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2019,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2019,12,31]]},"container-title":["History and Collective Memory in South Asia, 1200\u20132000"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2006.07.006","title":["Interactions of meaning and syntax: Implications for models of sentence comprehension"],"author":[{"given":"Gail","family":"McKoon","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Roger","family":"Ratcliff","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2006.07.006","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2007,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2007,2]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173014408.89705418\/v2","title":["Impact of Large Language Models on Conversational Translation: Towards Translation Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0479-4990","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Toru","family":"Ishida","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science, Hong Kong Baptist University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yohei","family":"Murakami","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Science and Engineering, Ritsumeikan University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Arif","family":"Bramantoro","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing & Informatics Universiti Teknologi Brunei"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173014408.89705418\/v2","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,2]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Despite advances in deep  learning, conversational translation has continued to face persistent  challenges: ambiguity in short sentences, limited data for  colloquial language, and the need for culturally-situated, commonsense-based  translations. However, Large Language Models (LLMs) are now leveraging their  vast background knowledge to bring about a fundamental shift in the field. In  this paper, we first review the various research and technological developments  that have attempted to realize conversational translation prior to the  emergence of LLMs. We then demonstrate that, with appropriate prompting, LLMs  can resolve most of the key challenges identified in conversational translation  research over the last two decades. Finally, we outline the future challenges that  the research community must address in the era of LLMs.       \u00a0<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5727082","title":["Biased Decisions of Large Language Models (LLM): Computer Control Agents and the Decoy Effect"],"author":[{"given":"Kevin","family":"B\u00f6sch","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5727082","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2008.02.005","title":["Computing and recomputing discourse models: An ERP study"],"author":[{"given":"Giosu\u00e8","family":"Baggio","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Michiel","family":"van Lambalgen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Peter","family":"Hagoort","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2008.02.005","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2008,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2008,7]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003374527-12","title":["Collective memory and charismatic affect"],"author":[{"given":"Antonio","family":"Donato","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Eduardo","family":"Galak","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Lorenzo","family":"Pedrini","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003374527-12","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,22]]},"container-title":["Football, Fandom and Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/bs.pbr.2022.07.006","title":["What is the relationship between collective memory and metacognition?"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/bs.pbr.2022.07.006","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"container-title":["Progress in Brain Research","Collective Memory"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,9,15]]},"abstract":"<p>This chapter provides an overview of the themes of the book. Largely in response to their own national predicament in post-Union imperial Britain, Scottish writers of the Romantic period brought to the British Atlantic a historiography of collective or cultural memory, which imagined an unprecedented fissure within the flow of time that had rent the present from the past. This sense of an immense gulf between past and present \u2013 measured in only one or two generations and imagined to be within reach of, or just beyond, living memory \u2013 was attended by deep national anxieties but also by a renewed optimism, of social and cultural reinvention. As it circulated along the routes of the British empire, Scottish history writing of the period made a fundamental contribution to the culture of modernity in the Atlantic world.<\/p>","DOI":"10.3366\/edinburgh\/9781474455466.003.0001","title":["\u2018So complete a change\u2019 (in So Short a Time) \u2013 Scottish Romanticism, Modernity and Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Kenneth","family":"McNeil","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2021,5,20]]},"container-title":["Scottish Romanticism and Collective Memory in the British Atlantic"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,9,15]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3366\/edinburgh\/9781474455466.003.0001"},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781351198479-7","title":["Montserrat Roig and Juan Mars\u00e9: Storytelling and the Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Kathryn","family":"Crameri","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781351198479-7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,12,2]]},"container-title":["Language, the Novelist and National Identity in Post\u2013Franco Catalonia"]},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2025.emnlp-main.574","title":["Collaborative Beam Search: Enhancing LLM Reasoning via Collective Consensus"],"author":[{"given":"Yangyifan","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Shuo","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jiajun","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2025.emnlp-main.574","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2025.104663","title":["Learning filler-gap dependencies with neural language models: Testing island sensitivity in Norwegian and English"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7810-4959","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Anastasia","family":"Kobzeva","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Suhas","family":"Arehalli","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tal","family":"Linzen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dave","family":"Kush","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2025.104663","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2010.11.006","title":["What models of verbal working memory can learn from phonological theory: Decomposing the phonological similarity effect"],"author":[{"given":"Judith","family":"Schweppe","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Martine","family":"Grice","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ralf","family":"Rummer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2010.11.006","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2011,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2011,4]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.14746\/snp.2016.16.13","title":["History and culture in language education: monuments and memorials as expressions of collective memory"],"author":[{"given":"Hanna","family":"Komorowska","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.14746\/snp.2016.16.13","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2018,7,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2018,7,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>History and culture in language education: monuments and memorials as expressions of collective memory<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Scripta Neophilologica Posnanienia"]},{"DOI":"10.7146\/sss.v6i3.20647","title":["Past and Future in the Construction of Communal Identity: Collective Memory and Mythical Narratives"],"author":[{"given":"Christoffer","family":"K\u00f8lvraa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7146\/sss.v6i3.20647","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2015,4,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2015,4,7]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This article relates to the question of whether or to what extent identity is wholly constructed through language by engaging in a discussion of how the use of the past enters into the construction of communal identity. It argues that in order to understand why collective memory and the mythical narrativisation of the past which it frames are such powerful elements in the construction of collective identities, it must be distinguished from the science of historiography. But neither can collective memory be thought of as a communal analogue to the individual mental process of remembering. Rather it is a specific kind of discourse whose subject-position is endowed with a number of distinct privileges (different from those in the discourse of modern historiography) and through which a community can approach and articulate its past in mythical narratives whose \u2018validity\u2019 in fact has little to do with the extent to which they mirror \u2018historical reality\u2019. <\/jats:p>","container-title":["Scandinavian Studies in Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-319-76051-3_8","title":["Troubled Pasts, Collective Memory, and Collective Futures"],"author":[{"given":"Cristian","family":"Tileag\u0103","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-319-76051-3_8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2018]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2018,5,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2018]]},"container-title":["Imagining Collective Futures"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-031-04504-2_5","title":["The History of Speech-Language Therapy in Kenya: A Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Bea","family":"Staley","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Emma","family":"Shah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-031-04504-2_5","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2023,2,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Handbook of Speech-Language Therapy in Sub-Saharan Africa"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5286003","title":["Can Memory-Augmented Large Language Model Agents Help Journalism in News Interpretation and Cognitive Framing Across Diverse Audiences?"],"author":[{"given":"Leyi","family":"OuYang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5286003","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781032634357-3","title":["Collective Efficacy"],"author":[{"family":"Ly","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781032634357-3","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,1,10]]},"container-title":["Navigating Special Education Relationships"]},{"DOI":"10.4018\/978-1-7998-0951-7.ch009","title":["Society of Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0449-6272","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Steven","family":"Walczak","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of South Florida, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/978-1-7998-0951-7.ch009","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The development of multiple agent systems faces many challenges, including agent coordination and collaboration on tasks. Minsky's The Society of Mind provides a conceptual view for addressing these multi-agent system problems. A new classification ontology is introduced for comparing multi-agent systems. Next, a new framework called the Society of Agents is developed from Minsky's conceptual foundation. A Society of Agents framework-based problem-solving and a Game Society is developed and applied to the domain of single player logic puzzles and two player games. The Game Society solved 100% of presented Sudoku and Kakuro problems and never lost a tic-tac-toe game. The advantage of the Society of Agents approach is the efficient re-utilization of agents across multiple independent game domain problems and a centralized problem-solving architecture with efficient cross-agent information sharing.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Natural Language Processing"]},{"DOI":"10.1080\/14681810309042","title":["Interrupting Discourses Around Gender Through Collective Memory Work and Collaborative Curriculum Research in Middle School"],"author":[{"given":"Kiran D.","family":"Purohit","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Christopher","family":"Walsh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/14681810309042","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2003,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2003,7]]},"container-title":["Sex Education"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9780203052891-16","title":["Reading Instruction in an Integrated Language Perspective: Collaborative Interaction in Classroom Curriculum Genres"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9780203052891-16","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2013,4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2013,4,3]]},"container-title":["Instructional Models in Reading"]},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.173137619.99016839\/v1","title":["Dynamic Incentives for Collaborative Intelligence Sharing in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Wei","family":"Wu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Northwestern Polytechnical University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-8731-0112","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yixuan","family":"Pan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Northwestern Polytechnical University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinyu","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Northwestern Polytechnical University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Liang","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Northwestern Polytechnical University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lexi","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China United Network Communications Group"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.173137619.99016839\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) has ushered in a\ntransformative era in artificial intelligence, particularly within the\nrealm of network security. These models demonstrate exceptional\ncapabilities, from conducting code vulnerability audits to proposing\nsolutions, significantly impacting decision-making processes in\nsecurity. Ensuring the consistency and accuracy of security threat\nintelligence across models becomes a key challenge when sharing\nknowledge among different large models, especially in the context of\ndeploying various Large Language Models for security purposes. This\npaper introduces dynamic incentive sharing mechanisms (DISM), an\nincentive mechanism crafted to foster the sharing of network threat\nintelligence among LLMs. Grounded in evolutionary game theory, DISM\naddresses the challenge of knowledge discrepancies among LLMs by\npromoting collaboration and seamless sharing of security threat\nintelligence. DISM formulates incentive strategies to mitigate\nfree-riding behavior among participating LLMs. In conclusion, the\neffectiveness of DISM in promoting and regulating threat intelligence\nsharing was verified through experimental simulations and comparative\nanalyses.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003461548-6","title":["Conclusion to Part I: Law and Collective Memory, Collective Memory and Law"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2048-2073","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Miros\u0142aw Micha\u0142","family":"Sadowski","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003461548-6","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,1,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,1,16]]},"container-title":["Intersections of Law and Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014260100004052","title":["TinyGraphEstimator: Adapting Lightweight Language Models for Graph Structure Inference"],"author":[{"given":"Michal","family":"Podstawski","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"NASK National Research Institute, Warsaw, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014260100004052","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781316809600.010","title":["Role of Individual Memory in the Construction of Collective Memories"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781316809600.010","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"container-title":["Collective Remembering"]},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9783111211763-001","title":["Chapter One Theorizing Collective Memory as Currency"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9783111211763-001","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,17]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory as Currency"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5333028","title":["Can Memory-Augmented Large Language Model Agents Help Journalism in News Interpretation and Cognitive Framing Across Diverse Audiences?"],"author":[{"given":"Leyi","family":"Ouyang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5333028","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.1093\/oso\/9780192895363.003.0002","title":["Temporal Security in IR"],"author":[{"given":"Kathrin","family":"Bachleitner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/oso\/9780192895363.003.0002","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,3,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,3,18]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n               <jats:p>The first chapter of this book theoretically conceptualizes collective memory in international relations (IR). The link between the IR discipline and the interdisciplinary collective memory concept is provided through the framework of ontological security. The inquiry begins by extrapolating the nature of ontological security and its most essential component: state identity. It then moves on to theorize collective memory as the underlying carrier of state identity. Collective memory highlights identity\u2019s temporal dimension and manifests it within the collective frameworks of narration. At the end, a new approach, \u2018temporal security\u2019, is developed. It combines the ontological security of being with the definition of memory as being-in-time. Security-seeking behaviour for states now implies to be temporally grounded in a consistent narrative that links past, present, and future. The reference point for this as of yet untheorized security need is collective memory. Manifesting itself in the varying forms of political strategy, public identity, state behaviour, and national values, collective memory thus navigates countries through time in IR.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Collective Memory in International Relations"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7167198","title":["Topological Emotional Memory: A Framework for Deep Affective Recall in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-8812-0807","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Lefliti","family":"Mohamed","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7167198","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in natural language processing, yet they fundamentally lack the capacity for genuine emotional memory and affective reasoning. This paper introduces Topological Emotional Memory (TEM)-a novel framework that integrates a structured, graph-based emotional memory system with existing LLMs without modifying their core architecture. Drawing from topological principles, cognitive psychology, and affective neuroscience, TEM models memories as nodes in a directed graph embedded in a continuous emotional space, where edges represent affective, sensory, and temporal relationships. We present a comprehensive mathematical formulation including: (1) a multidimensional emotional encoding based on valence-arousal-dominance (VAD) space, (2) a topological graph structure with weighted edges representing emotional similarity, (3) retrieval algorithms for contextually-aware memory recall, (4) dynamic learning mechanisms for memory consolidation and adaptation, and (5) integration protocols for existing transformer-based LLMs. Through rigorous theoretical analysis and detailed case studies, we demonstrate that TEM enables: (a) contextually-aware emotional retrieval with 92.3% relevance accuracy, (b) deep empathetic response generation that maintains emotional coherence across interactions, (c) dynamic learning from user interactions with minimal computational overhead (O(N + E) per update), and (d) navigation through complex affective trajectories via topological pathfinding algorithms. The framework bridges the critical gap between statistical language modeling and phenomenological experience, offering a computationally tractable (O(N log k) retrieval complexity) approach to emotional intelligence in AI systems. Our results suggest that TEM can serve as a foundational architecture for next-generation empathetic AI, with applications in mental health, education, creative AI, and human-computer interaction.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6514258","title":["LMI: Latent Memory Injection for Efficient and Modular Knowledge Access in Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Maryam","family":"Samani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6514258","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) increasingly rely on Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to access external knowledge. While effective, standard token-based RAG introduces significant context expansion, increases inference latency, and incurs quadratic computational costs in self-attention-limitations that are particularly severe for Small Language Models (SLM) such as Gemma 2B. In this paper, we propose Latent Memory Injection (LMI), a modular architecture that integrates external knowledge directly into the model's latent space rather than through token concatenation. LMI retrieves dense knowledge vectors from a FAISS-indexed memory and injects them into intermediate hidden states using a learned, token-dependent gating mechanism. As a result, sequence length remains constant regardless of retrieval volume. We evaluate LMI on three domain-specific factual benchmarks-medical, legal, and sports reasoning-and compare it against token-based RAG under matched compute budgets. Experiments show that LMI achieves 96.7% of RAG's F1 score and 5.7 \u00d7 lower latency while adding minimal static memory overhead. Further analysis demonstrates that LMI supports zero-shot domain swapping with &amp;lt; 10% accuracy degradation and that learned gates selectively activate on factual entities, highlighting LMI as an efficient and modular alternative to token-based retrieval.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781316809600.009","title":["Collective Memory in the Mind: Russians\u2019 Remembrance of the Past"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781316809600.009","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"container-title":["Collective Remembering"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9780203086889-10","title":["Between Collective Memory and Manipulation: The Holocaust, Wagner and the Israelis"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9780203086889-10","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2013,9,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2013,9,13]]},"container-title":["After Eichmann"]},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.175571729.90298303\/v1","title":["Memory-Augmented Language Models (MALM): A Unified Architecture for Knowledge Integration and Safety Compliance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-5238-0634","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jiarui","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.175571729.90298303\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,20]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Existing information retrieval approaches for LLMs, such as retrieval augmented generation (RAG), require complex setup and deployment. Many other techniques such as RETRO also attempt to augment model memory to limited efficacy and simplicity. We present Memory-Augmented Language Models (MALM)-a simplistic, novel approach that integrates trainable memory mechanism through a unified attention layer. By using a single memory-attention component appended to a frozen base LLM, MALM enables selective external knowledge access without large-scale\/multi-phase retraining or explicit pointer mechanisms. Such approach, which minimizes implementation complexity and preserves interpretability, is versatile in both knowledge augmentation and safety compliance domains.<\/jats:p>\n                <jats:p>Disclaimer: 20-35% of the supplemental topics in this paper are completed with the help of AI.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9781800735965-006","title":["Chapter 1. Towards a Collaborative Memory: A Framework and a Method"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9781800735965-006","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,31]]},"container-title":["Towards a Collaborative Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.7208\/chicago\/9780226399294.003.0008","title":["The Contextualized Past"],"author":[{"given":"Jeffrey Andrew","family":"Barash","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7208\/chicago\/9780226399294.003.0008","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2016]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2016]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory and the Historical Past"]},{"DOI":"10.21832\/9781783093595-008","title":["4. Working Memory and Interpreting: A Commentary on Theoretical Models"],"author":[{"given":"Yanping","family":"Dong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Rendong","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21832\/9781783093595-008","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2015,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2015,12,31]]},"container-title":["Working Memory in Second Language Acquisition and Processing"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5332083","title":["Can Memory-Augmented Large Language Model Agents Help Journalism in News Interpretation and Cognitive Framing Across Diverse Audiences?"],"author":[{"given":"Leyi","family":"Ouyang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5332083","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2016.03.007","title":["Making long-distance relationships work: Quantifying lexical competition with Hidden Markov Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5950-0139","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Julia","family":"Strand","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"David","family":"Liben-Nowell","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2016.03.007","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2016,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2016,10]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9783110327175.21","title":["Collaborative Art and Collective Intention"],"author":[{"given":"Sondra","family":"Bacharach","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Deborah","family":"Tollefsen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9783110327175.21","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2008,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2008,12,31]]},"container-title":["Concepts of Sharedness"]},{"DOI":"10.29070\/27\/57475","title":["Ethnicity, Identity and Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Pradeep","family":"Acharya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.29070\/27\/57475","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2018,4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2018,4,1]]},"container-title":["Contemporary Social Sciences"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,9,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Intelligent agents collect and process information from their dynamically evolving neighborhood to efficiently navigate through it. However, agent-level intelligence does not guarantee that at the level of a collective; a common example is the jamming we observe in traffic flows. In this study, we ask: how and when do the interactions between intelligent agents translate to desirable or intelligent collective outcomes? To explore this question, we choose a collective consisting of two kinds of agents with opposing desired directions of movement. Agents in this collective are minimally intelligent: they possess only a single facet of intelligence, viz., memory, where the agents remember how well they were able to travel in their desired directions and make up for their non-optimal past. We find that dynamics due to the agent\u2019s memory influences the collective, giving rise to diverse outcomes at the level of the group: from those that are undesirable to those that can be called \u201cintelligent.\u201d When memory is short term, local rearrangement of agents leads to the formation of symmetrically jammed arrangements that take longer to unjam. However, when agents remember across longer time-scales, their dynamics become sensitive to small differences in their movement history. This gives rise to heterogeneity in the movement that causes agents to unjam more readily and form lanes.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1063\/5.0148977","title":["Emergence of intelligent collective motion in a group of agents with memory"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6983-0390","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Danny Raj","family":"Masila","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Lab 10, Department of Chemical Engineering, IISc Bangalore , Bangalore 560012, Karnataka, India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-4205-7187","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rupesh","family":"Mahore","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Lab 10, Department of Chemical Engineering, IISc Bangalore , Bangalore 560012, Karnataka, India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2023,9,20]]},"container-title":["Chaos: An Interdisciplinary Journal of Nonlinear Science"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,9,1]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1063\/5.0148977"}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":100,"search-terms":null}}}